{"headline":"You missed half your sessions this week, and your lifts have stalled — time to refocus.","summary":"This was a tough week. You completed Upper A and Lower A but missed Upper B and Lower B, meaning you got through only half of your planned training. With just 16 total sets across the week, your volume is well below what's needed to drive muscle growth for a 28-year-old male at your training level. On the bright side, the sets you did complete were solid in terms of load, and your strength indexes held steady across all muscle groups — nothing is going backwards. The foundation is still there; the priority now is getting back to full attendance and reigniting progression on your main lifts.","progressiveOverload":{"status":"stalling","notes":"Every muscle group's strength index is completely flat this period compared to last: chest held at 104, back at 103, shoulders at 102, biceps at 101, and triceps at 103. Legs actually dipped slightly from 106 to 105, which is the only directional change and a mild one. No group showed any upward movement. This stalling pattern is consistent with the missed sessions — you simply didn't accumulate enough stimulus to drive adaptation. Progressive overload requires consistent, sufficient volume, and with two full sessions missed, the body had little reason to get stronger."},"muscleImbalances":[{"muscleGroup":"shoulders","severity":"mild","observation":"Shoulders received only 2 sets and account for just 9.4% of weekly volume, which is on the low side relative to the chest and back volume. With two sessions missed this week, shoulder accessory work likely suffered most, and this gap may widen if Upper B continues to be skipped."},{"muscleGroup":"biceps","severity":"mild","observation":"Biceps make up only 4.8% of total volume with 2 sets, the lowest share of any group. For a muscle-building goal, this is below the recommended minimum effective volume, and the flat strength index at 101 confirms no meaningful stimulus was applied."}],"deloadRecommended":false,"deloadReason":null,"actionItems":["Prioritize full routine attendance next week — completing all four sessions (Upper A, Lower A, Upper B, Lower B) is the single most impactful thing you can do to get muscle growth back on track.","On your Upper B session, deliberately add a small amount of load or one extra rep on your main chest and back movements to break the current strength index plateau on those groups.","Add at least one additional set each for biceps and shoulders to bring their weekly volume up to a more effective range for hypertrophy — even a single extra working set per session makes a difference at this volume.","In the days before your next training block, review what caused the two missed sessions this week and address that barrier directly, whether it's scheduling, fatigue, or motivation, so you can protect your consistency going forward."],"contextSummary":"User completed only 2 of 4 planned sessions (Upper A and Lower A), resulting in 16 total sets, flat strength indexes across all muscle groups, and no progressive overload; full attendance and small load increases are the top priorities next period."}